; `(absent)` with nothing to be absent.
;
; The sibling `empty_absent.ein` is `(absent ?x)`, a guard over no premise:
; it would fail against every KB, and its candidates would be retired
; permanently. With no argument at all the hazard is the same: read as an
; ordinary relation, `absent` would match the empty extent of a relation
; nobody declared and retire the rule for the run in silence. The grammar
; refuses it at the parse, at the closing parenthesis (Q-M1e.18: `absent`
; reads exactly one premise).
;
; So the two files are one hazard at two arities, and both are refused at
; the parse.
(relation p T)
(relation q T)

(rule guard-of-nothing ()
  :match  (and (p ?x) (absent))
  :assert (q ?x))
